        "math_academy_id": "q-116122",
        "knowledge_point_id": "345d6df7-2ada-5c2e-8146-7c779afae3af",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form",
        "problem": "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$-3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-24x+8y=41?$",
        "worked_solution": "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-24x+8y & =41 \\\\ {y}^{2}+8y-3{x}^{2}-24x & =41 \\\\ [{y}^{2}+8y]-3[{x}^{2}+8x] & =41 \\\\ [(y+4)^{2}-16]-3[(x+4)^{2}-16] & =41 \\\\ (y+4)^{2}-16-3(x+4)^{2}+48 & =41 \\\\ (y+4)^{2}-3(x+4)^{2} & =41+16-48 \\\\ (y+4)^{2}-3(x+4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-(-4))^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(-4,-4).$",
