      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-76108",
        "knowledge_point_id": "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions",
        "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)={2}^{x}+1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5).$",
        "worked_solution": "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(5).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(5)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(5) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =5 \\\\ {2}^{x}+1 & =5 \\\\ {2}^{x} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{x} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(5)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x}+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices_complete": true,
