      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-77452",
        "knowledge_point_id": "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14094255/assets/1649e6c4ef2874cda2a7d7428b54220ef2a1790ba4b197c755165c48add0c31e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
        "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14094255/assets/7cf17440c27765efb34b6a01c12a76ad4e980fc540307e2218227153661db5dd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2.5|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2.5a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=3|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2.5a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=2.5a+3b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n$\\,$ $\\lambda =2.5$ and $\\mu =3$",
