        "math_academy_id": "q-113501",
        "knowledge_point_id": "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858",
        "knowledge_point": "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain",
        "problem": "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4x+3}{5x+2k}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{5})\\cup (\\frac{2}{5},\\infty ).$ What is the value of $k?$",
        "worked_solution": "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2k & =0 \\\\ 5x & =-2k \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{2k}{5}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{2k}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2k}{5} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{k}{5} & =-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
