        "knowledge_point_id": "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero",
        "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{0}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z-0}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{0}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=-z,\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+1}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-1:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1+2t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+1}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-1}{2}=-z,\\,x=-1\n$$",
