      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-127519",
        "knowledge_point_id": "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14094392/assets/30299b98c822a5b06b332860bafbed4f465a4567a7135dc3257c4c9e40e490fa.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above.",
        "worked_solution": "We know that the line passes through the points $P(2,-3,4)$ and $Q(-1,-3,7).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-1,-3,7⟩-⟨2,-3,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-3}=\\frac{y-(-3)}{0}=\\frac{z-4}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-3}=\\frac{y+3}{0}=\\frac{z-4}{3}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\frac{y+3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=-3.$ For example, our two points $P(2,-3,4)$ and $Q(-1,-3,7)$ both have $y$-coordinates of $y=-3.$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y+3}{0}$ part and instead write $y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{3},\\,y=-3.\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
